{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_60","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'git diff 8e3009b2c7305cd94500009875ddc1b7880d8331 5690b25710615f5b0f9aba1f2642eaa6a917ccd9 -- POSE.md .pose/templates/roadmap.md pose-mcp/schemas/v1/review-plan.schema.json'","aggregated_output":"diff --git a/.pose/templates/roadmap.md b/.pose/templates/roadmap.md\nindex 54699c8b..a234d003 100644\n--- a/.pose/templates/roadmap.md\n+++ b/.pose/templates/roadmap.md\n@@ -3,6 +3,7 @@ slug: <roadmap-slug>\n status: draft        # draft | active | done | abandoned\n created_at: <YYYY-MM-DD>\n depends_on:          # prerequisite roadmaps, inline list: other-roadmap-a, other-roadmap-b\n+adrs:                # optional ADR associations, inline list: [adr:<file>.md] (closeout review policy, when-justified)\n ---\n \n # Roadmap: <roadmap-slug>\ndiff --git a/POSE.md b/POSE.md\nindex 404ee8cb..04242fda 100644\n--- a/POSE.md\n+++ b/POSE.md\n@@ -371,6 +371,7 @@ pose release-package-manifests --version X.Y.Z --checksums <checksums.txt> --out\n   **Raw history:** every history reader of structural review and of the delivery graph ignores replace refs and grafts; a repository with either gets other review inputs than before, so rerun `pose index` and reseal. A trailer change set that starts at the repository's first commit resolves against the empty tree.\n   **Removal witnesses:** a review subject path a later spec removed reads as that removal only while Git still shows it: the deleting commit on `HEAD`, read raw, with no later commit touching the path, in a clone that is not shallow. Otherwise preparation refuses and asks for `pose index`. A generation binds the change sets of `.pose/reports/history` as `recorded-change-sets`, so one published earlier reads as unbound until the next `pose index`.\n   **Children:** a milestone of one local member has no review of its own and stands on that spec's closed approval, and a roadmap may declare `unreviewed_milestones` for milestones whose members are all terminal, sealed by its own review. Otherwise a milestone or roadmap's approval stands only while the review of each child does: a child rejected, stale, with lost attestation history, or not closed under a done parent stops the parent verifying approved, in `review verify`, `review-check` and closeout, and the parent recovers with its child, with no reseal. Selection follows replacement lineage: a replacement of the same content comes before what it replaces whatever the seal times say, a `replaces` naming other content counts for nothing, and a replaced bundle's approval is not retained.\n+  **Closeout review policy (schema 5):** an instance that adopts `pose adopt closeout-review-policy` decides with `milestone_review_required` and `roadmap_review_required` (`never`, `when-justified`, `always`) whether the close of a milestone or a roadmap owes its own review. `when-justified` requires it for a milestone with more than two distinct specs, and for a roadmap with more than one distinct spec and at least one ADR association (`adrs: [adr:<file>.md]` on the roadmap, `roadmaps: [roadmap:<slug>]` on the ADR, or a local Markdown link either way). `always` requires it even for a one-member milestone, and neither the singleton inheritance nor `unreviewed_milestones` weakens it. Member reviews stay required; a review recorded on the scope anyway is verified as before and its rejection stands; a member or association that cannot be resolved never grants an exemption. Under the adopted policy `pose close milestone:<roadmap>/<id>` and `pose close roadmap:<slug>` stay explicit in every mode and publish a receipt (`review-closeouts/<milestone|roadmap>/…json` inside `.pose/`) with `review: required|not-required`, the key, the value, `closeout-review@1` and the reason (`members=1, threshold>2`); the scope is terminal only with it. New instances adopt `never`/`never`; an existing policy keeps the legacy rule until it adopts, `pose setup` shows both, `pose doctor` lists open scopes that owe a review, and roadmaps closed before adoption are verified by the contract they were sealed under.\n - `close` — applies a review-gated lifecycle transition to complete a spec, milestone, or roadmap. The transition is published only over the bytes its gates judged, under the spec's lifecycle lock that start and transfer also take (spec `pose-writer-coordination-and-atomic-publication`): an edit made while the gates ran is a `publish-conflict` that keeps the edit, the authorization is judged again inside the lock, and a start or transfer holding the spec refuses the close with the lock's owner (`start-in-progress` or `spec-lifecycle-in-progress`). Git state (spec `pose-git-and-dispatch-snapshot-integrity`): a `git status`, HEAD or tree that cannot be read is unknown, not clean — the close plan blocks on it rather than sealing evidence over a working tree it could not see (a directory with no repository at all is a different fact and keeps its behaviour); the governed snapshot, the review-bundle path check and the obligation snapshot report an unreadable status as unknown too. `review dispatch` compares the review worktree with the sealed commit's own tree before and after the run, so `assume-unchanged`, `skip-worktree`, a replaced type or a link cannot hide an edit; native derived regeneration requires exact producer outputs that remain regular files; adapter output belongs on stdout or the external output path, and directory names grant no permission to modify the review worktree, and a run that finishes after its bundle was superseded or its policy changed is recorded `stale`, history and not the scope's current fulfilment.\n - `continuous-closeout` — persists and projects continuous closeout terminal state for rapid-iteration workflows. `status --json` writes one operation result whose `data` holds `active` and `terminal`; the exit stays 1 while an active scope is not terminal.\n - **Output channels** — the result (verdict, findings, data) goes to stdout and progress (steps, usage, the command's own failures) to stderr, so `pose x --json | jq` is safe and `2>/dev/null` never hides a finding. `--json` prints one document whose finding `severity` is the untranslated anchor; `--quiet` prints the verdict alone; `pose validate --verbose` streams a check's output instead of capturing it. Colour, symbols and the spinner exist only on a terminal, resolved per stream, and honour `--color`, `POSE_COLOR`, `NO_COLOR` and `TERM`; `COLUMNS` sets the wrap width and `POSE_LOCALE` (`en` or `pt-BR`) the language of every message POSE composes. Exit codes: 0 pass, 1 gate failure, 2 usage error.\n@@ -899,6 +900,10 @@ versus task and follow native next actions.\n \n `pose review` governs the subject (bundle, brief, attestation, verify); `pose delegate` governs executions. An **agent** is a CLI profile configured in `.pose/policy/delegation.json`; a **run** is one execution; an **attempt** is a run and its continuations; a **thread** is the rounds of one scope. `pose delegate review`, `adjudicate` and `smoke` run the sealed bundle's generated brief on a disposable worktree at the sealed commit with read access only, under the attempt ledger and containment below; any other `--access` is refused. The agent never records an attestation: the engine records the conclusion of a completed review run only as the conclusions of delegated reviews below describe. Without `--agent` (or with `auto`) routing chooses among the agents of the purpose: first by `routing.prefer` (default `opposite-vendor-of-implementation`, in the order the policy declares its agents), then by the steps of `routing.on_unavailable` (default `same-vendor-other-model`, `wait`, `same-model`). `wait` applies only when every earlier candidate is limited with a known reset within `routing.max_wait` (default 5h) and starts the run after the first reset, without polling. The implementation routed against is the identity the bundle sealed; for a bundle sealed before bundles recorded it, the policy's `provenance.implementation`, else `--lead`. An agent named with `--agent` bypasses routing and warns when it shares the implementation's vendor. Every run records the decision (`routing`: the candidates, why each was skipped, the step taken) and `independence_achieved` (`different-vendor`, `different-model` or `same-model`); with no agent available the run does not start and the error carries the decision. `--label` names the run, `--lead <vendor>[/<model>]` declares the implementation when the bundle records none, `--dry-run` prints the resolved agent, purpose, access, working directory and argv (authorized credential values redacted) and reserves nothing, and `--json` prints one versioned document (`pose.delegate.run/v1`, `plan/v1`, `ls/v1`, `cancel/v1`, `agents/v1`, `error/v1`), usage errors included. On `ls`, `resume`, `cancel` and `agents` the same flags select: a run or an agent that does not match is not listed, or is refused when named; `--dry-run` changes nothing anywhere. Messages follow `POSE_LOCALE` (`en` or `pt-BR`). A run reference is a run id, an attempt id, a label or a session prefix of six characters or more; an ambiguous reference exits 2 and lists the candidates.\n \n+A schema-2 agent may opt in to `review_profile.command` and `review_profile.resume_command` for governed review only. Omission preserves its existing read adapter; tasks, adjudication and smoke keep their commands. A resumable agent must declare the profile's resumed command with `{session}`. Once selected, failure never falls back to the read or task profile. The selected commands remain bound by policy/provenance verification. Older strict parsers reject the new field. The configured Claude event codec canonicalizes repeated typed init records only for the same parsed session identity before session recognition. Different identities and generic regex ambiguity are still refused; raw provenance/redaction and limit/reset channels retain the original bytes.\n+\n+This repository's Claude review profile uses safe/restricted mode, Read/Grep/Glob/Bash only, mandatory shell sandboxing (`failIfUnavailable:true`, `allowUnsandboxedCommands:false`), and a read-only sealed source tree. Both fresh and resumed wrappers require bubblewrap, socat and jq on Linux (jq remains required on macOS), with installation advice on refusal. Go cache/temp writes go to private operation-owned scratch outside the checkout; the existing module cache is read, and unavailable downloads/tests that write into source can fail. The profile instructs the reviewer to run `go clean -cache` as its final Bash command inside the sandbox; the engine independently retains incomplete cleanup as a failed run under its unchanged limits. Never clean an adapter-controlled cache outside the sandbox: a test can replace its path with a symlink. This qualifies Claude's shell subprocess boundary, not isolation of the trusted Claude executable or arbitrary credential reads. Checkout integrity checks still apply. Installing engine code does not adopt the profile in another instance; copy and qualify its explicit policy block before use. The wrapper also denies the resolved physical Git common directory, because Claude auto-allows it for worktrees. For this opt-in profile, POSE prepares only empty `.claude/.cc-writes` directories at the disposable checkout root and declared components before taking its integrity snapshot; added files and all later mutations still fail. Final sandboxed cleanup includes leftover `go-build*` directories directly under GOTMPDIR after all jobs finish.\n+\n `pose delegate ls` lists attempts by their latest run with state, purpose, agent, label, turns, age and last step — for an interrupted run also its cause, whether a partial draft exists, the reset instant and the time left — and the runs in progress with the liveness of their process. `pose delegate cancel <run>` stops a run in progress; it is recorded `failed` with the cause `cancelled`, and its attempt stays consumed. `pose delegate agents` lists each agent with vendor, model, whether its CLI is on the path, the reset instant of its last recorded usage limit and the state routing reads: `available`, `limited until <instant>` (its last run hit a usage limit whose reset is ahead), `missing` (its CLI is not on the path), `unknown` (the run history could not be read whole) or `probe-failed` (its optional `probe` failed within its bound). Availability is read from the path and the recorded runs; only a configured probe runs anything. `pose delegate review|adjudicate|smoke --detach` starts the run as a process of its own, in a session of its own that survives the caller's terminal, and returns at once with the attempt (a run reference) and the path of its log; a run whose route must first wait for a usage-limit reset is not detached. `pose delegate wait <run> [--timeout <d>] [--json]` is a plain foreground process that ends when the run does and prints one line — run, state, decision, resumable flag, reset instant — with the exit code `0` approved, `10` changes requested or rejected, `20` failed (a completed run with no decision too), `30` interrupted, `40` stalled, `50` the wait timed out, and `2` for a usage error; every waiter is released, a run already ended returns at once, and killing a wait leaves the run running. The process running an attempt rewrites a heartbeat in its marker every 10 seconds; a run whose heartbeat is older than three intervals and whose process is provably gone is recorded `stalled` by the first reader, through an exclusive claim every reader then agrees on, and `ls` shows each running run's heartbeat age. `pose delegate watch [<run>|<label>]` shows an attempt as one task: the runs it recorded are replayed with a marker where a run resumed another and the interrupted run naming the run that continued it, then a running attempt is followed through the normalized, redacted events its process writes as they come, until the record is published; `--no-follow` prints what exists and exits, `-v` adds the tail of each tool's output (`--lines <n>`, default 12) and `--json` prints NDJSON. With no reference it follows the only running run; at a terminal it otherwise opens fzf with a preview of the run under the cursor refreshed every `--refresh <d>` (default 2s, 0 turns it off), or a numbered menu without fzf; outside a terminal an absent reference exits 2 and lists the candidates. Colours appear only on a terminal and follow `NO_COLOR`. A live event is redacted with the credentials and the session references the stream has declared so far; the events bound to the record are derived again from the whole transcript. `on_terminal` on an agent (or else on a purpose) is a command run once after a run ends, with only `POSE_DELEGATE_RUN` and `POSE_DELEGATE_STATE` in its environment, the run directory as working directory, its own process group and a bound (`timeout`, default 30s, at most 5m); its failure is recorded beside the run, shown by `ls`, and never changes the run's state. `pose delegate handoff <run> --to <agent|auto>` continues a run that stopped interrupted or failed, short of a conclusion, with another agent under the same attempt and its continuation budget, which is the `resume.max_resumes` and `max_wall_time` of the agent that opened the attempt (3 and 60 minutes by default) whichever agent continues it, so changing agents never widens it; a completed run, the same agent and a run already continued are refused, and a run handed off is no longer resumed. Its brief is the predecessor's generated brief followed by a labelled section: the predecessor's partial final message and the commands it ran with their exit status, paired by the call identifier the agent's stream records and otherwise left unknown rather than guessed, each with its digest, quoted so no line of it reads as a decision, bounded at 16 KiB with the cut disclosed, and an instruction to re-verify. Only the handoff run concludes. The record binds the base brief digest and the section digest in `handoff_digest`, names its predecessor in `hands_off_from`, and a signed proof verifies the section against the predecessor's record. `--to auto` routes as the purpose does, the stopped agent excluded and unavailable ones skipped. A foreground run or handoff that stops interrupted in a terminal offers waiting for its reset and resuming, handing off, or cancelling, which is offered even alone, and does nothing until answered; the stopping command's result, `ls --json` and `wait --json` carry the same ways forward as `next_actions`, each with its command, whether it is available now — judged by the planning its command runs, budget and wait bound included — and why not, and a `cancel` that leaves the run stopped. `watch` marks a handoff with `⇄` beside the `↻` of a resume. `pose delegate task <prompt-file|->` hands any task to another agent: the prompt goes on stdin in `--cd` (default: the current directory) with the agent's command for `--access read|sandboxed|write|full`; the default is `purposes.task.default_access` (read when unset), `full` is never a default and prints a warning naming what it disables. A task's template places `{add_dirs}`, `{no_network}` and `{outside_git}`, filled from the agent's `add_dir_args` (with `{dir}`), `no_network_args` and `outside_git_args`; `--add-dir`, `--no-network` and `--effort` that the agent's template cannot carry are refused with exit 2, and `--no-network` with `write` or `full` too. The final message goes to stdout and the agent's exit code is returned; `--json` replaces both with one document. A task runs in the real directory: containment bounds its time and output, not its filesystem. Its record (prompt, stream, error stream, normalized events, argv, removed variable names, exit code, label) is owner-only under the user's state directory (`$XDG_STATE_HOME/pose/delegate/`, where `XDG_STATE_HOME` counts only as an absolute path and `~/.local/state` is the default), or, with `--record`, in the `delegate-runs` directory of `.pose`. The state directory may be reached through a link of the user's own; resolved, it and every directory above it must belong to the user or to root and be writable by no group or other user unless sticky, and `pose`, `pose/delegate` and the project's directory below it must be real directories of the user's that no group or other user can write; a task refuses to record or read anywhere else and names the directory (on Windows only links are refused). `--detach` starts a task as the review purposes start a detached run and returns its attempt, its log and the place of its record; a prompt piped to `task -` or `resume --prompt -` is read before detaching and written to the detached run through a pipe, never a file, and a refusal before the attempt is reserved returns the foreground's document and exit code. A detached call keeps its log and hand-back in `.pose/review-private/supervisors`, which, with every directory up to the project root, must be the user's and writable by no group or other user, and whose root's ancestors must belong to the user or root and be writable by no one else unless sticky; the root is resolved once and only that resolved root is used, a caller whose current directory is gone is refused, and a project's private task history is keyed by its resolved root (records an earlier engine kept under a linked spelling are still read). A detached call believes a hand-back only once a piped prompt is through and a run reserved the attempt it names. A checkout that fails this can still run in the foreground; on Windows only links and non-directories are refused, and ACLs are not checked. A task is never evidence: no review reader reads it, `pose review attest --run` refuses it by name, and `wait` returns 0 when the agent succeeded, with no decision. `ls`, `watch`, `wait`, `cancel`, `resume` and `handoff` work on tasks; `resume` without `--prompt` sends a default continuation message, with the agent's `resume.commands` for the task's access, and `--agent auto` routes against the launching session. A task can also continue a session POSE never recorded, such as one a personal launcher opened, with `--session <id> --agent <name>`: the agent's resume command for the access runs with that session, the prompt optional, and the record says it continued a session without keeping it. `pose review dispatch`, `resume` and `status`, the aliases this surface deprecated, were removed: each exits 2 and names its `pose delegate` replacement.\n \n `delegation.json` is schema 2: `agents` (vendor, model, `cli`, `events`, per-access `commands`, `effort`, `timeout`, `limit`, `session`, `resume`, `executor`, `containment`, `on_terminal`, `probe` — `command` and an optional `timeout` up to 60s, default 10s, with no placeholder), `routing` (`prefer`, `on_unavailable`, `lead_models` as `{vendor: model}` in lower case, `max_wait` up to 24h), per-purpose budgets (`purposes.<review|adjudicate|smoke|task>.max_runs_per_bundle`, `timeout`) and `env_unset`. Command templates are argv arrays; `{output}`, `{session}` and `{effort}` are whole elements, never interpolated, and `{effort}` becomes the argv fragment of the agent's effort level. An agent's own `max_runs_per_bundle` wins over the purpose's; a purpose's `timeout` wins over the agent's. `pose update` converts `reviewers.json` (schema 1) into `delegation.json` deterministically and removes it: each adapter becomes an agent whose `read` command is the adapter's command, a budget all adapters share moves to the review-path purposes, and every agent projects exactly the adapter it came from. The converted file is archived outside `.pose/policy` and named in `migrated_from`, so a run recorded before the conversion keeps its policy baseline while `delegation.json` is exactly that conversion; editing `delegation.json` ends that, as any policy change does. The engine reads `reviewers.json` while `delegation.json` is absent and refuses when both exist. A schema 1 adapter that interpolates `{output}` or `{session}` inside an argument (in `sh -c` code, for example) is not converted: `pose update` warns and keeps `reviewers.json` until the template passes the placeholder as a whole element.\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/schemas/v1/review-plan.schema.json b/pose-mcp/schemas/v1/review-plan.schema.json\nindex 2dafaa89..dc28d2d5 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/schemas/v1/review-plan.schema.json\n+++ b/pose-mcp/schemas/v1/review-plan.schema.json\n@@ -21,6 +21,7 @@\n     \"bands\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/band\"}},\n     \"projection\": {\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/projection\"},\n     \"policy_baseline\": {\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/policyBaseline\"},\n+    \"closeout_review\": {\"$ref\": \"closeout-receipt.schema.json#/$defs/decision\"},\n     \"structure\": {\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/structure\"},\n     \"warnings\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}},\n     \"blockers\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}},\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
